WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.231 --> 00:00:02.010
(soft intro music)

00:00:02.010 --> 00:00:04.530
- [Antony] What we have here
is the Hudson Valley Fisheries

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:06.810
cold smoked steelhead trout.

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:08.280
- [Narrator] The site of fish on a plane

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:10.170
might be worrying for some,

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:13.380
especially on a flight
that's going to last 19 hours

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:15.600
- [Antony] Just layering this up nicely.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:17.490
- [Narrator] But not for Antony McNeil.

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:18.690
- [Antony] I'm okay with it

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:20.220
because the product's super fresh.

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:21.390
It's super tasty.

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:23.130
- [Narrator] A chef with a
background in hospitality,

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:23.963
McNeil is now

00:00:23.963 --> 00:00:26.280
the Food and Beverage Director
for Singapore Airlines

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:29.670
where he oversees the creation
of the 280 unique dishes

00:00:29.670 --> 00:00:31.380
the airline offers each month.

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:33.570
- So that's our first class salad.

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:35.550
- [Narrator] But how does
this smoke trout salad

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:38.070
get to this tray table exactly?

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:40.230
McNeil is here to explain the logistics

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:42.990
behind flying this fish and how he uses it

00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:44.100
to create a recipe

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:46.743
made for some of the
world's longest flights.

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:53.550
Singapore Airlines operates
four ultra-long haul routes,

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:55.680
including two 19 hour trips

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:58.320
from JFK, or Newark, to Singapore.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:00.540
Getting food prepared
for these long journeys

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:02.280
is no small feat.

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:03.681
- [Antony] From Newark to Singapore,

00:01:03.681 --> 00:01:08.681
we feature around 450, 460
different meal combinations

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:12.390
from the meals which
are supplied on board.

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:14.400
So whether it be the starter,

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:15.660
the three, four choices

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:17.919
that you have for the main course option,

00:01:17.919 --> 00:01:20.130
desserts, dinner, cheese.

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:21.060
So this is Singapore Airlines'

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:22.530
first class caviar service.

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:24.540
- [Narrator] Sourcing the
ingredients for these options

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:28.410
is a balance of both timing and taste.

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:30.990
This salad tells that story.

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:33.060
First, timing.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:36.060
To see why McNeil calls
this steelhead trout fresh,

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:38.130
it's best to look at a map.

00:01:38.130 --> 00:01:40.110
This is John F. Kennedy Airport.

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:41.310
This is Newark.

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:43.050
Both have daily Singapore Airlines flights

00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:46.890
that carry trout raised here
at Hudson Valley Fisheries.

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:50.820
The fish farm is located about
130 miles from each airport.

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:53.100
- [Antony] Some catering
partners or suppliers

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:56.010
may fly the steelhead trout
from Norway or from Chile.

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:57.060
So you're talking

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:59.760
three and a 3,500 miles to 5,000 miles

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:02.117
from these points of
destination into the country.

00:02:02.117 --> 00:02:03.900
- [Narrator] McNeil estimates that journey

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:06.450
could add days to the
fish's total transit time.

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:08.280
Whereas, the trout from the Hudson Valley

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:10.823
doesn't have to fly to fly.

00:02:10.823 --> 00:02:12.600
- [Antony] And again, very simply,

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:15.150
we're just gonna layer
that into the salad.

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:16.590
- [Narrator] The trout used in this dish,

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:18.840
which McNeil is assembling
in a test kitchen,

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:22.380
goes from tank to plane in under a week.

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:24.090
It's first harvested at the farm

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:26.040
and sent to a smokehouse nearby.

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:28.200
After that, it's sliced and packaged,

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:29.100
which is when the clock

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:31.620
for Singapore Airlines really begins.

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:32.460
- [Antony] It's sent off

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:35.040
to our catering partners
in JFK and Newark.

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:38.790
So within receipt of the goods
into the catering facility,

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:41.250
generally, you would see that
flying onboard the aircraft

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:44.190
within 24, 36 hours of that product

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:46.680
arriving from Hudson Valley Fisheries.

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:49.380
Topping this off with a spicy watercress.

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:50.970
- [Narrator] The greens used in this salad

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:53.250
are on the plate in even less time,

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:56.430
around 24 hours from
when they're harvested.

00:02:56.430 --> 00:02:58.320
That's because they come from AeroFarms,

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:00.150
an indoor vertical farm in Newark,

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:02.220
less than five miles from the airport.

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:03.300
- [Antony] We're really maximizing

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:05.190
the freshness and the
closeness of the supply

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:06.630
to the finished plate.

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:11.630
I'm just gonna finish that off,
around, and over the salad.

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:14.100
- [Narrator] But getting
the ingredients to the plane

00:03:14.100 --> 00:03:16.260
is only part of the process.

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:17.883
Next, is taste.

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:21.360
- [Antony] We're just gonna take the trout

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:24.630
and we're just going to pull
it and so that it flakes.

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:26.490
- [Narrator] Creating
a travel ready recipe

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:28.980
is different from building
a restaurant ready meal,

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:31.920
because it's being served
in a pressurized cabin,

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:35.910
which changes how passengers
taste and feel when they eat.

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:37.830
Take the smoke steelhead, for example.

00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:40.000
Singapore Airlines worked
with Hudson Valley Fisheries

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:42.600
to get the flavor right for flight.

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:44.730
- [Antony] When we initially
reviewed the steelhead trout,

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:46.560
the feedback that we gave the team

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:49.560
was that it was slightly
too salty for our needs.

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:50.393
- [Narrator] Salt is something

00:03:50.393 --> 00:03:53.100
airlines tend to load up on
to give food more flavor,

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:56.580
because travelers taste buds
dull in the arid, dry plane.

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:59.580
But too much salt can
also lead to dehydration,

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:01.830
making for an uncomfortable trip.

00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:03.930
So the flavor comes from elsewhere.

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:05.040
- [Antony] This is the dressing.

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:08.580
So this is a tomato vinaigrette
with a ras el hanout spice.

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:11.790
So the tomato is giving
us some umami flavor

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:12.660
and then the ras el hanout

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:14.400
will give us a little bit of heat.

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:15.233
- [Narrator] Of course,

00:04:15.233 --> 00:04:17.700
McNeil isn't the one
assembling travelers' meals.

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:19.230
Once the recipes are set,

00:04:19.230 --> 00:04:21.960
they're given to airport
catering companies to prepare.

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:23.460
- They're detailed line-by-line.

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:25.110
Each ingredients clearly identified

00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:26.190
where the weight requirement

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:29.580
and we have a photograph
depending on the cabin class

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:32.220
that you're flying for
the caterers to work to

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:33.180
in terms of, you know,

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:36.000
where to put what
ingredients and sauces where.

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:36.840
- [Narrator] Meals could be dished

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:38.730
six to eight hours ahead of the flight,

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:40.560
after which they are
held in chilling fridges

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:43.530
before being put on carts
and lifted onto the plane.

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:46.500
From there, the crew is
given plating instructions.

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:48.210
- Now this is on a first class plate.

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:49.680
- [Narrator] The order of the ingredients,

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:52.740
radicchio, beetroot
leaves, trout, radishes,

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:55.740
and their placement on the
plate are all predetermined.

00:04:55.740 --> 00:04:58.578
- [Antony] Here we have
the roasted beetroot puree.

00:04:58.578 --> 00:05:00.990
Just add that in at the end of the dish.

00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:03.990
- [Narrator] Properly plated,
the meal is ready to eat.

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:06.720
And that chain that started
in the Hudson Valley

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:10.323
is finally finished
somewhere at 30,000 feet.

